<h2>The allegation and the arrest</h2>
<p>The FIR, registered at the Cyber Crime Police Station, Patan, alleged that the accused posed as telecom and IPS officials and, under threat of arrest, induced the complainant to transfer about Rs 67 lakh — a pattern now commonly described as a digital-arrest fraud. The charges span cheating, impersonation, forgery-related and organised-crime provisions of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, together with Section 66C of the Information Technology Act.</p>
<p>The applicants were arrested from Uttar Pradesh. The Patan police recorded the arrest on one date while the papers showed detention from the previous day; the accused were produced before the Chief Judicial Magistrate, Patan — hundreds of kilometres from the place of arrest — without any transit remand from a Uttar Pradesh Magistrate, and beyond twenty-four hours of actual detention. The grounds of arrest were furnished in writing, but in Gujarati; the arrestees, from Uttar Pradesh, had signed their papers in Hindi.</p>

<h2>The constitutional measuring rod</h2>
<p>The Court tested the arrest against Articles 21 and 22 of the Constitution and the arrest provisions of the BNSS, including Section 47, which requires that the grounds of arrest be communicated — and communicated meaningfully, in a language the arrestee understands. The investigating officer admitted the lapses. On those admitted facts, the Court held the arrest and continued detention illegal, and followed the course indicated by the Supreme Court decision it relied upon (Jaskaran Jeet Singh Deol v. State of Punjab, 2026 INSC 1022): release, compensation, and departmental accountability for the officers concerned.</p>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>The safeguards that were breached</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Transit remand from a Magistrate at the place of arrest before carrying the arrestee to another State</li>
<li>Production before a Magistrate within twenty-four hours of actual detention, not paper arrest</li>
<li>Written grounds of arrest in a language the arrestee understands</li>
<li>Arrest memo attested by the arrestee or a family member</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Why this matters beyond Gujarat</h2>
<p>Inter-state arrests are now routine in cyber-fraud investigations: the complainant is in one State, the money trail in a second, the accused in a third. Delhi Police teams travel out of the NCT for such arrests every week, and teams from other States arrest in Delhi just as often. The discipline this judgment enforces is therefore of daily relevance in Delhi practice. An arrestee picked up in Delhi for an outstation FIR is entitled to be produced before a Delhi Magistrate for transit remand, and that hearing is a real safeguard: counsel can appear, point out defects, and in appropriate cases the Magistrate can refuse transit custody.</p>
<p>Equally significant is the Court's refusal to let subsequent remands launder the original illegality. Prosecutions often argue that once a Magistrate has remanded the accused, earlier defects are academic. The judgment holds the opposite: a later formal arrest or remand cannot cure an earlier unlawful detention. For bail practitioners, that makes the first forty-eight hours of custody a permanent part of the case record — worth documenting precisely, with timelines, memos and language details, even if the bail application comes months later.</p>

<h2>The limits of the relief</h2>
<p>The order is carefully bounded. The Court said nothing on the merits; the trial proceeds; and the release is on conditions the trial court may impose, with release barred if the applicants are required in another case. The compensation of Rs 25,000 per applicant is modest and symbolic — but its source is not: it is to be recovered from the responsible officers, a direction designed to make arrest discipline self-enforcing.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This report is based on the order of the Gujarat High Court in Jagdishkumar Harilal Prajapati v. State of Gujarat, Criminal Misc. Application No. 16603 of 2026, dated 7 October 2026. The arrest safeguards discussed — grounds of arrest, twenty-four-hour production and remand — are uniform across India under the BNSS and the Constitution, and apply with equal force in the courts of Delhi.</p>
</div>
